\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Self-Consistency and the Condorcet Independence Assumption}
\label{sec:condorcet}

Self-Consistency \citep[SC;][]{wang2023sc} generates $K$ reasoning traces by sampling a language model at temperature $T$ and selects the majority answer: $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \sum_{i=1}^{K} \mathbf{1}[a_i = a]$. This procedure instantiates the Condorcet Jury Theorem \citep{condorcet1785}: if $K$ independent voters each choose the correct answer with probability $p > 0.5$, the majority converges to the correct answer as $K \to \infty$. The theorem's central requirement is \emph{voter independence} \citep{austensmith1996}.

Same-model sampling violates this requirement. All $K$ traces share the same parameters, the same prompt, and differ only through sampling randomness. \citet{ladha1992correlated} showed that correlated voters reduce majority-rule effectiveness. We quantify this correlation using Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa}, which measures inter-rater agreement across $K$ traces on $n$ problems. We define the \emph{Effective-K}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
\text{Eff-}K = \frac{K}{1 + (K - 1) \cdot \kappa},
\end{equation}
which represents the number of truly independent traces that $K$ correlated samples provide. When $\kappa = 0$ (full independence), $\text{Eff-}K = K$; when $\kappa \to 1$ (full agreement), $\text{Eff-}K \to 1$. In our experiments (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), we observe $\kappa = 0.40\text{--}0.74$ across model-domain conditions, yielding $\text{Eff-}K = 1.2\text{--}2.2$ for nominal $K = 12$. This means adding traces beyond Eff-$K$ provides no new information for either majority voting or constraint-based aggregation.

\subsection{SICA Pipeline}
\label{sec:sica-pipeline}

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) augments SC with a constraint satisfaction stage. Given a question $q$, SICA proceeds in four stages (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}):

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\small
